 "result": "Use `@app.cli.command()`. It's the decorator you want in most cases.\n\n- **Decorator:** `app.cli` is an `AppGroup` (`src/tessel/app.py:338`). Its `command()` wraps your callback in `needs_app_scope` automatically (`src/tessel/cli.py:413-427`). That gives the command a loaded app and an active app context. Commands on `bundle.cli` get the same treatment.\n- **Standalone commands:** if you define a plain `@click.command()` instead, add `@needs_app_scope` yourself (`src/tessel/cli.py:380`). You can also use `@AppGroup().command()`.\n- **App loading:** the `LaunchInfo` class in `src/tessel/cli.py:293` does it, through `LaunchInfo.load_app()` (line 333). It uses `create_app`, then the `--app` / `TESSEL_APP` import path, then `wsgi.py` or `app.py` in the current directory. `TesselGroup` (line 531) creates it and uses it to look up commands under `app.cli`.\n- **Plugin commands:** commands registered under the `tessel.commands` entry point group (line 604) are not tied to an app. They load without one, so they need `@needs_app_scope` if they use the app.",
